# One row per document the ingest loads, whatever it attests.
#
# The set of documents is a choice
#   The set of documents is a choice somebody makes on purpose, and this file is one of the
#   three answers. Every query downstream is "some relation, over some set of documents".
#   Composing a scope/ relation is what makes that choice a line somebody wrote rather than a
#   `WHERE` clause somebody could forget. This is the widest answer: take everything that
#   loaded.
#
# Both a slot value and a relation composed directly
#   The two call sites are worth comparing. `matrices.sqlc` passes it into
#   `entries/coupling_presence.sqlc`'s `@scope`; `queries/matrices/1-fit-from-ranges.sqlc`
#   composes it as an ordinary join. Same file, same rows, and the slot form is the one where
#   forgetting to choose fails to compose.
#
# The scope a rule runs under, and a report must not
#   A fixture is a stipulation: it exists to put the model in a state the corpus never reached,
#   so a rule must be tested against it and a finding about the world must not count it. Choose
#   scope/corpus.sqlc for anything that says "in this corpus".
#
# The one scope that carries `evidence_absent`
#   A document may decline to say what it is evidence for, and then it belongs to neither of the
#   other two scopes, each of which keeps one stated value. So only this relation can hold the
#   reason it declined, and it carries it.
#
# And the author of an assertion
#   A composition names the party that composed it and the standing it composes on. That is a fact
#   about the document, so it is here, empty on a filing, which has no author of its own.
-- from pm.filing: both evidence values, the typed reason a document gives neither, and an assertion's provenance.
SELECT f.name AS filing, f.kind, f.evidence, f.evidence_absent,
       f.prov_party, f.prov_entered_by, f.prov_approved_by,
       f.prov_standing_taxonomy, f.prov_standing_value, f.prov_standing_absent, f.prov_note
FROM pm.filing f
